      "rationale": "More, fewer, and same-number relations all occur as keys, and the square-triangle, triangle-rectangle and square-rectangle pairings are each sampled at least once."
    },
    "cognitive_capacity": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Offering 'they are different' as a choice answers a different question than the one asked. A first grader reading 'Which shape has the same number of sides as a square?' must discard an option that is not a shape at all, which adds a logical hurdle the competency never asked for."
    },
    "variant_comprehensiveness": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Nine of ten renders use the identical two-shape comparison sentence. Only the 'tilted square' item changes the frame by bringing orientation in, and nothing asks a pupil to sort or group shapes by a feature."
    },
    "competency_alignment": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "The 'fewer corners' question recurs four times with only option order shuffled, so seed variation is spending itself on reshuffling instead of on finer feature discrimination such as equal sides versus unequal sides."
    },
